    "title": "Women are making an impact on male dominated industries - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By McKenzie Jarrell ,\n1 hours ago\nHARRISBURG, Pa (WHTM) — Women are starting to make an impact in industries that were once — and in some cases still are — male dominated.\n“We are breaking barriers and there is a place for us in this industry,” said Fawn Cassel, an engineer with PennDOT.\n“I started at the Turnpike Commission nine years ago,” she said. “I was one of maybe three female engineers in the department. And since I’ve been there over the last nine years, you know, we’ve grown the department in the female presence, not only in our department, but within the organization, as well.”\n‘I didn’t have dreams before’: 17-year-old Pennsylvania girl finds new family In an effort to get more women involved in the industry, Cassel launched ‘ S.T.E.A.M Day ’ – an event geared towards young women.\nThe fourth annual event was held Wednesday at the Pennsylvania Farm Show Complex in Harrisburg.\n“It’s all girls from local Midstate high schools,” she said. “The girls are becoming more involved in whether it’s civil engineering, aerospace, you know, into the fields that were always predominantly targeted by men.”\nGirls — like Evelyn Dryden, a senior at Trinity high school in Cumberland County.\n“I get to see different women in the fields that I want to be in, which is like engineering and transportation,” Dryden told abc27 News.\nThe girls also get the opportunity to explore these careers firsthand through different activities.\n“I came with the dream of doing a S.T.E.A.M Day for and giving these girls this opportunity.”\nDownload the abc27 News+ app on your Roku, Amazon Fire TV Stick, and Apple TV devices\nS.T.E.A.M stands for science, technology, engineering, art and math.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to ABC27.",
